1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,171
What if Claude edited a file you never wanted it to touch, like the file with your passwords and API keys?

2
00:00:06,621 --> 00:00:10,746
In this video, you'll learn how to stop that for good, with Claude Code hooks.

3
00:00:11,196 --> 00:00:16,950
I'll explain what a hook is, when to use one, and then we'll build a real one together, step by step.

4
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:19,351
So, what is a hook?

5
00:00:19,801 --> 00:00:24,142
A hook is a command that Claude Code runs automatically, at a moment you choose.

6
00:00:24,592 --> 00:00:32,609
Those moments are called events. For example: just before Claude uses a tool, right after it edits a file, or when it finishes answering.

7
00:00:33,059 --> 00:00:38,259
Your command can simply let things carry on. Or it can block the action, and tell Claude why.

8
00:00:38,709 --> 00:00:43,812
You set hooks up in a settings file, with a few lines of JSON, like the ones on the right.

9
00:00:45,262 --> 00:00:47,449
Here's how a hook works, in three parts.

10
00:00:47,899 --> 00:00:53,990
First, an event and a matcher. For example: just before Claude uses the Edit or Write tool.

11
00:00:54,440 --> 00:01:00,406
Second, your script runs. Claude Code sends it the details, like which file Claude wants to change.

12
00:01:00,856 --> 00:01:09,870
Third, the result. Exit with code zero, and Claude carries on. Exit with code two, and the action is blocked, and Claude reads your reason.

13
00:01:10,320 --> 00:01:16,219
And here's the key point: a hook runs every single time. It doesn't depend on Claude remembering a rule.

14
00:01:17,669 --> 00:01:21,045
So when should you use a hook, instead of just writing an instruction?

15
00:01:21,495 --> 00:01:29,941
An instruction in CLAUDE.md is guidance. Claude usually follows it, but it's not guaranteed. That's perfect for style and preferences.

16
00:01:30,391 --> 00:01:37,412
A hook is code that runs every time. Use it for rules that must always hold, or actions that must always happen.

17
00:01:38,862 --> 00:01:41,823
There are many events. Here are four you'll use the most.

18
00:01:42,273 --> 00:01:46,491
PreToolUse runs before a tool, so it can check the action, or block it.

19
00:01:46,941 --> 00:01:52,807
PostToolUse runs after a tool succeeds. For example, to format a file Claude just edited.

20
00:01:53,257 --> 00:01:57,713
Notification runs when Claude needs your attention, so you can get a desktop alert.

21
00:01:58,163 --> 00:02:00,437
And Stop runs when Claude finishes answering.

22
00:02:01,887 --> 00:02:04,015
Our example: protecting your secrets.

23
00:02:04,465 --> 00:02:10,796
Most projects have a dot env file, with passwords and API keys. Claude should never change it.

24
00:02:11,246 --> 00:02:18,042
With a PreToolUse hook, any attempt to edit it is blocked, Claude explains why, and every other file works as normal.

25
00:02:19,592 --> 00:02:25,332
First, the problem. Here's a small Python project, with my secrets in a dot env file.

26
00:02:25,782 --> 00:02:30,402
I've turned on auto-accept for edits, so Claude can work without asking me each time.

27
00:02:30,852 --> 00:02:34,072
Now I ask: clean up the formatting in my config files.

28
00:02:34,522 --> 00:02:40,893
Claude edited the dot env file too, where my secret keys live. It was trying to help, but I didn't want that.

29
00:02:41,343 --> 00:02:43,342
Let's make sure this can never happen again.

30
00:02:44,192 --> 00:02:46,047
Step one: write the hook script.

31
00:02:46,497 --> 00:02:50,623
I create a hooks folder inside dot claude, with a small Python file.

32
00:02:51,073 --> 00:02:56,809
At the top, I list what Claude must never edit: the dot env file, and a secrets folder.

33
00:02:57,259 --> 00:03:04,679
When the hook runs, Claude Code sends the details of the tool call as JSON. I read the file path Claude wants to change.

34
00:03:05,129 --> 00:03:10,937
This line turns Windows backslashes into forward slashes, so the check works on every computer.

35
00:03:11,387 --> 00:03:17,042
Then the check. If the path matches a protected pattern, I print a reason, and exit with code two.

36
00:03:17,492 --> 00:03:21,499
Otherwise, exit with code zero, and Claude carries on as usual.

37
00:03:21,949 --> 00:03:25,600
Before wiring it up, I test it by hand, with a fake tool call.

38
00:03:26,050 --> 00:03:29,923
It prints the reason. And the exit code is two. The script works.

39
00:03:30,773 --> 00:03:33,296
Step two: register the hook in your settings.

40
00:03:33,746 --> 00:03:38,710
In the project's .claude folder, I open settings.json, and add a hooks section.

41
00:03:41,846 --> 00:03:46,135
The event is PreToolUse, so it runs just before Claude uses a tool.

42
00:03:46,585 --> 00:03:52,069
The matcher says which tools: only Edit and Write, the tools Claude uses to change files.

43
00:03:52,519 --> 00:03:58,525
And the command runs my script with Python. The project dir variable always points to the project folder.

44
00:03:58,975 --> 00:04:02,645
On a Mac or Linux, you might need to write python3 instead.

45
00:04:03,095 --> 00:04:09,458
Where you save the hook decides who it protects. This project's settings file can be committed, so your whole team gets it.

46
00:04:10,408 --> 00:04:11,801
Step three: test it.

47
00:04:12,251 --> 00:04:17,297
I start Claude Code again, still with auto-accept on, and repeat the same request.

48
00:04:17,747 --> 00:04:22,726
Claude tries to edit dot env, and the hook blocks it, before anything changes.

49
00:04:23,176 --> 00:04:26,736
Claude reads the reason, explains it to me, and moves on.

50
00:04:27,186 --> 00:04:30,999
Even with auto-accept turned on, the hook still has the final word.

51
00:04:31,749 --> 00:04:37,402
And normal files still work. I ask for a docstring in app dot py, and the edit goes through.

52
00:04:38,952 --> 00:04:41,755
Step four: go further. Here are four tips.

53
00:04:42,205 --> 00:04:46,536
One: type slash hooks in Claude Code, to see every hook it has loaded.

54
00:04:46,986 --> 00:04:53,950
Two: always test your script on its own first, like we did, by piping in sample JSON and checking the exit code.

55
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:58,947
Three: commit the dot claude folder to git, and your whole team is protected.

56
00:04:59,397 --> 00:05:07,790
Four: try other events. A PostToolUse hook can format every file Claude edits, and a Notification hook can alert you when Claude needs you.

57
00:05:09,340 --> 00:05:09,994
Let's recap.

58
00:05:10,444 --> 00:05:14,818
A hook is a command that Claude Code runs automatically, at an event you choose.

59
00:05:15,268 --> 00:05:20,936
Exit with code zero to carry on, or code two to block the action, and tell Claude why.

60
00:05:21,386 --> 00:05:25,655
Use instructions for preferences, and hooks for rules that must always hold.

61
00:05:27,105 --> 00:05:32,603
The hook script and settings from this video are linked in the description, so you can copy them and try it today.

62
00:05:33,053 --> 00:05:39,884
If this helped, like the video, and subscribe to Zero Cost AI for more free AI tutorials. See you in the next one!

